Title:
ResetNightmare (CVE-2026-27912) Proof-of-Concept PowerShell Script

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
ResetNightmare is a PowerShell POC that automates exploitation of CVE-2026-27912, a validation flaw in the Kerberos Change Password protocol enabling an attacker to reset passwords for arbitrary AD user or computer accounts without knowing the current password. The attack relies on an unpatched Domain Controller and the attacker’s ability to set (write) a `userPrincipalName` on an account they control, or to create a new user/computer object (which grants GenericWrite over the created object). The script performs the full flow: UPN manipulation to impersonate the target name, requesting a TGT using `NT-ENTERPRISE` to the `kadmin/changepw` SPN, then using that ticket to reset the target’s password. It uses Rubeus for Kerberos ticket operations and the ActiveDirectory PowerShell module for directory changes and cleanup. This is primarily useful for red teams and pentesters assessing AD privilege escalation paths and for defenders validating exposure and patch status. It’s important because it turns relatively common delegated AD permissions (UPN write / object creation) into direct account takeover via password reset.

Technical Focus:
- Kerberos Change Password protocol (`kadmin/changepw`) abuse
- `userPrincipalName` (UPN) attribute manipulation and name resolution
- TGT request with `NT-ENTERPRISE` name type
- Active Directory permissions: GenericWrite / delegated object creation in OUs
- Rubeus-based Kerberos ticket acquisition and usage
- Password reset of user and computer (`sAMAccountName` / trailing `$`) accounts

Use Cases:
- Validate whether domain controllers are vulnerable/unpatched to CVE-2026-27912
- Demonstrate AD account takeover from delegated UPN-write permissions
- Abuse “create user/computer in OU” delegations to pivot into password resets
- Red team simulation of domain compromise via Kerberos password-change path
- Blue team reproduction for incident response, detection engineering, and remediation testing

Keywords:
CVE-2026-27912, ResetNightmare, Active Directory, Kerberos, Change Password protocol, kadmin/changepw, userPrincipalName, UPN spoofing, sAMAccountName, NT-ENTERPRISE, TGT, Rubeus, PowerShell, ActiveDirectory module, GenericWrite, OU delegation, user creation, computer account, password reset, domain controller unpatched